  "gui.gonzotech.notes.title": "Scholar's Notes",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.locked": "Locked — keep researching",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.illustration": "illustration",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.chapter_era1": "Era I",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.chapter_era2": "Era II",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.chapter_era3": "Era III",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.chapter_era4": "Era IV",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.chapter_era5": "Era V",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p1.title": "Hello me",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p1.body": "*\"How do I put this... I — that is, you — am the old you. I wiped my, well, your memory. I don't plan on coming back: you're my living tombstone now. I hope you get a better life than I had... Good luck.\"*\n\nSelf — Gonzo:\n\nFarewell, Gonzo...\n\nTo bring order to this mad world, the first thing I crafted was a chalkboard. It is My chief instrument of knowledge. Now I can see formulas before My eyes, balance equations, and make discoveries.\n\n**By dragging blocks into an equation**, I immediately see the board's hints — it judges how precisely the elements are balanced.\n\nIf the balance accuracy **exceeds 90%**, it results in a full **Discovery**.\n\nHere I can leave Myself notes and endlessly combine units, deriving new physical quantities. As I progress, the board will unlock harder problems and unknown constants.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p2.title": "Observation",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p2.body": "Watching the wild beasts, I noticed how they leave fruit in pits or foliage before eating it. There must be something to it. Ripening?",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p3.title": "Fermentation",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p4.title": "Energy",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p4.body": "Watching how flame devours wood and how water rushes irresistibly downward, I matched up the elements of this equation. It seems I have grasped a remarkable pattern… There is hidden power in everything. One need only make it flow in the right direction.\n\nSo as not to get lost in My own conclusions, I coined new names for Myself. From now on **GTH** (Gonzo tech Heat) shall stand for heat, and **GTU** (Gonzo tech Units) for pure energy. Life is more pleasant when the chaos around you takes on strict names...\n\nMy first step: to learn to extract pure **GTH** from controlled combustion. For this I built the firebox. Let us see whether it can hold this fury.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p5.title": "Firebox and Boiler",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p6.title": "Stirling Generator",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p6.body": "The symbiosis of firebox and boiler exceeded all My expectations, but steam is only an intermediate link. To turn raging **GTH** into useful **GTU**, I needed something more complex. I assembled a Stirling engine. By colliding the heat of hot steam with the cold of the surrounding world inside it, I set the pistons moving. Mechanical magic in its purest form. Yet this triumph came with a most irritating price. The boiler is incredibly voracious. Water evaporates at a frightening rate, and I have to literally stand watch by the vats, constantly topping up buckets so the system doesn't stall. I must urgently figure out how to automate this flow. My hands are already calloused from the buckets.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p7.title": "Condensation",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p7.body": "Endlessly hauling buckets is the lot of savages, but never Mine. I pondered long over where the steam goes, and the answer was in plain sight. Why keep seeking new sources when you can loop the old ones? I think I found the solution: returning the escaping moisture back into the system is far more profitable than endlessly pouring in water. I built the first condenser. Scalding steam, striking its cold inner walls, turns back into droplets and trickles into the boiler. The circle is closed. Now it runs almost autonomously, and I can finally set aside the buckets and turn to something more important.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p8.title": "Closed Loop",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p8.body": "The condensers proved their worth, cutting losses, but they fight only the symptom, not the cause. Within a sealed system some error still remains. And then it dawned on Me: why save droplets when the surplus of generated energy can be directed to pump water straight from rivers and oceans?\n\nI invented the automatic pump. The consumer of energy became its own source, closing the equation. Now part of the **GTU** goes to powering mechanisms that ceaselessly pump water back into the boilers. It is a perfect symbiosis. I have literally made nature work for Me for free...",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p9.title": "Electric Smelting",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p9.body": "To leave accumulated **GTU** idle would be a crime against efficiency, but wasting it would be even more foolish. First I created the accumulator. And from it I powered My new creation — the electric furnace. Ordinary stone furnaces, devouring coal, now seem to Me a relic of the past. No smoke, no heat loss. Pure current, melting metal with perfect precision.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p10.title": "Logistics",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p10.body": "The first thing a true plumber needs is a good tool. To bring order to My blueprints and connect the scattered devices, I forged a heavy wrench. It is not just a lump of iron, but a full extension of My eyes and hands. On a normal look at nodes it lets Me precisely track the amount of matter and energy flowing inside the pipes. Tuning the network is now intuitive:\n\nA normal use (**RMB**) on a pipe cycles its mode: *automatic / pull / push*.\n\nA use while sneaking (**Shift + RMB**) lets you rotate elements and change their direction.\n\nTo save space, I designed the system so that all three kinds of conduit can fit compactly inside **a single block**: copper wires for **GTU**, fluid pipes for water or steam, and heat pipes for **GTH**. One turn of the wrench — and chaos bends to My will...\n\nMechanisms cannot exist in isolation — they need \"arteries\". To bind it all together, I had to develop a whole distribution system: copper wires to transmit **GTU**, fluid pipes to circulate icy water and steam, and thick mains capable of carrying **GTH**. Connecting them directly was not enough. I had to create special distribution nodes — junctions that obediently direct the flows of resources to wherever they are needed most.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p11.title": "Cobblestone Generator",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p11.body": "This machine is the crown of My automation. Consuming **GTU** and water, it begins to continuously synthesize stone and valuable ores. I could not make it 100% fail-proof, so I will have to watch for possible hiccups in its operation...",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p12.title": "Item Logistics",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p12.body": "I have developed item pipes, logistic nodes and two crucial control components — the filter and the scavenger. Now My resources can move between mechanisms and storages entirely on their own.\\n\\nThe logic of sorting and control:\\n\\nThe wrench is still My main tool. A press of **RMB** on a node changes its mode (*automatic / pull / push*).\\n\\nThe Filter (Sorter) lets through only those items I have selected in it. Everything else it blocks.\\n\\nThe Scavenger works in pair with the filter. It catches the surplus items the filter did not let through and automatically redirects them into other (alternative) pipes — for example, into a disposer or a trash chest.\\n\\nNow I can build a line that sorts out the haul on its own: what is needed goes to production, the unneeded goes to recycling, and everything else can be sent to a chest and forgotten.\\n\\nIt seems I have finally made chaos sort itself. A pleasant feeling.",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p13.title": "Item Logistics",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p14.title": "Universal Node",
  "gui.gonzotech.notes.p14.body": "Why build a complex system of different nodes, if you can make a single point in space handle absolutely everything? The Universal Node I designed solves this problem. All that is left is to figure out how to make it work in multithread in the future...",
  "item.gonzotech.the_proto_mash": "Proto-Mash",
  "item.gonzotech.the_fruit_mash": "Fruit Mash",
